---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import HeroSplit from '../../components/en/HeroSplit.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/en/CtaBand.astro';

const path = '/en/health-insurance-acupuncture';
const url = `https://tcm.ch${path}/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'I just moved to Switzerland - am I covered for acupuncture?',
    a: 'Your mandatory basic insurance only covers acupuncture when a doctor with complementary-medicine training performs it. For therapist-led TCM, like ours, reimbursement may be available through optional supplementary insurance for complementary medicine, depending on your policy, the treatment and the individual practitioner\'s recognition. If you only have basic cover, you likely need to add that supplementary product first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need supplementary insurance for TCM?',
    a: 'For therapist-delivered TCM, yes in practice. Basic insurance does not reimburse it. A complementary-medicine supplementary policy (Zusatzversicherung) is what may reimburse treatments at our clinics, and only when the treating practitioner holds a recognition your insurer accepts (usually EMR or ASCA). We tell you before your first appointment whether yours does.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I claim reimbursement?',
    a: 'You pay the clinic, receive an itemised invoice listing the EMR/ASCA registration, and submit it to your insurer - usually by app, post or online portal. Your insurer reimburses a share according to your policy. How much and how fast depends entirely on your tariff.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are your practitioners recognised by my insurer?',
    a: 'Recognition is practitioner-specific: EMR and ASCA are the registers most Swiss supplementary insurers reference for complementary medicine, and each policy sets its own terms. We tell you which recognition the practitioner treating you holds and help you check your specific cover before you book.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://tcm.ch/en/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Health insurance & acupuncture', item: url },
  ],
};

const insurers = ['CSS', 'Helsana', 'SWICA', 'Sanitas', 'Concordia', 'Visana', 'Groupe Mutuel', 'Sympany', 'KPT', 'AXA'];
---
<Layout
  title="Does Swiss Health Insurance Cover Acupuncture & TCM? | TCM.ch"
  description="Does Swiss health insurance cover acupuncture & TCM? A plain-English guide: basic vs supplementary cover, what EMR/ASCA means, how reimbursement works."
  path={path}
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <HeroSplit
    eyebrow="TCM.ch · For expats in Switzerland"
    h1="Does Swiss health insurance cover <em>acupuncture</em> and TCM?"
    lead="The Swiss system has two layers, and which one pays for acupuncture depends on who treats you. Here is the honest version."
    ctas={[{ href: '/en/contact/', label: 'Request an appointment' }, { href: '#faq', label: 'Jump to FAQ', kind: 'outline' }]}
    trust={['Supplementary insurance, not basic', 'Recognition checked per practitioner', 'We check your cover with you']}
    image={{ src: '/images/krankenkasse-hero.png', alt: 'Bright Swiss TCM practice with acupuncture needles and a tablet for insurance billing', caption: 'Illustrative image' }}
    crumbs={[{ href: '/en/', label: 'TCM.ch' }, { label: 'Insurance & costs' }]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="tldr">
        <strong>In short:</strong> Basic insurance (<em>Grundversicherung</em>) only covers acupuncture and TCM when a
        doctor with complementary-medicine training performs it. Therapist-delivered TCM - like at our clinics - is
        reimbursed, if at all, through supplementary insurance (<em>Zusatzversicherung</em>) for complementary medicine,
        depending on your policy, the treatment and the individual practitioner's recognition (EMR/ASCA). We tell you before your first appointment where you stand.
      </div>

      <h2>Basic vs. supplementary insurance</h2>
      <p>
        Almost everything that confuses newcomers comes down to this split. Swiss health cover is two separate things:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Basic insurance (Grundversicherung)</strong> - mandatory for everyone, identical benefits by law.
          It covers acupuncture only when delivered by a <em>physician</em> who has complementary-medicine training.
          It does <em>not</em> cover treatment by a non-physician TCM therapist.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Supplementary insurance (Zusatzversicherung)</strong> - optional, and you choose it separately.
          The complementary-medicine product is the one that reimburses therapist-led TCM, acupuncture, Tuina,
          cupping and herbal medicine at clinics like ours.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So if you've only ever signed the mandatory policy, you're probably not yet covered for our treatments. The fix
        is usually adding a complementary-medicine supplementary policy - ideally before you start treatment.
      </p>

      <h2>What is EMR / ASCA?</h2>
      <p>
        These are the two main Swiss recognition registers for complementary-medicine practitioners:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>EMR</strong> (ErfahrungsMedizinisches Register) - a quality register insurers rely on.</li>
        <li><strong>ASCA</strong> - a second, widely accepted recognition body.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Supplementary insurers reimburse treatment only when the practitioner is recognised by a register they accept.
        Recognition is practitioner-specific: before your first appointment we tell you which recognition the practitioner
        treating you holds, and each location page lists it per practitioner. It's the single most important box to tick:
        without it, even a good policy won't pay out.
      </p>

      <h2>How reimbursement works</h2>
      <p>You're not billed through your insurer up front. The flow is simple:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Book</strong> an appointment at one of our clinics.</li>
        <li><strong>Pay</strong> the practitioner directly after treatment.</li>
        <li><strong>Submit the invoice</strong> to your insurer - it shows the EMR/ASCA registration they look for.</li>
        <li><strong>Get reimbursed</strong> a share of the cost, per your supplementary tariff.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Reimbursement rates, annual ceilings and per-session caps are set by your individual policy - there's no single
        national rate. Check your tariff, or let us help you read it.
      </p>

      <h2>How to check your reimbursement in three questions</h2>
      <p>Call your insurer or check the policy document before the first session. Three questions settle most cases:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Does my supplementary plan cover complementary medicine</strong> (Komplementärmedizin), and which registers does it accept (EMR, ASCA, others)?</li>
        <li><strong>What share is reimbursed and what is the annual limit</strong> for complementary medicine?</li>
        <li><strong>Is a doctor's referral required?</strong> (For most supplementary products it is not, but some tariffs differ.)</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Then tell us the answers with your request: we match them against the practitioner's recognition before you commit. For a step-by-step walkthrough see <a class="inline-link" href="/en/knowledge/how-to-claim-acupuncture-on-swiss-insurance/">how to claim acupuncture on Swiss insurance</a>.</p>

      <h2>What the invoice must contain</h2>
      <p>Your itemised invoice is what the insurer reads. It lists your details, the treatment dates, the amounts, the tariff positions (treatment time under tariff 590) and the practitioner's name with the recognition number the insurer looks for. Keep it; a simple receipt is not enough.</p>

      <h2>Published treatment costs</h2>
      <p>Where our pages publish prices, they are the same in English: at the Basel clinic and at the partner practices in Kreuzlingen and Frauenfeld a <strong>follow-up session is typically around CHF 130 to 160</strong>, billed by treatment time under tariff 590. The first consultation takes longer because of the case history and costs accordingly more; herbal prescriptions are billed separately by formula. We tell you the exact amount before your appointment. Typical prices across Switzerland are discussed in <a class="inline-link" href="/en/knowledge/how-much-does-acupuncture-cost-in-switzerland/">how much acupuncture costs in Switzerland</a>.</p>

      <h2>Which insurers cover it?</h2>
      <p>
        Most major Swiss insurers offer a complementary-medicine supplementary product, among them
        {insurers.map((n, i) => (<>{i > 0 ? ', ' : ''}<strong>{n}</strong></>))} and others.
        What differs is the fine print: the percentage reimbursed, the annual cap and which registers they accept. We
        deliberately don't quote fixed percentages here, because they're tariff-dependent and change between products.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="inline-link" href="/krankenkassen/">See per-insurer details (German) →</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Ready to check?</h2>
      <p>Send your request with your insurer and policy model, or pick a location first: <a class="inline-link" href="/en/contact/">request an appointment</a>, <a class="inline-link" href="/en/acupuncture-near-me/">find the right clinic</a> or <a class="inline-link" href="/en/acupuncture-zurich/">acupuncture in Zürich</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/en/contact/">Request an appointment</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/en/acupuncture-near-me/">Find acupuncture near you →</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/en/locations/">See our clinics →</a>
      </div>

      <p class="disclaimer">
        This is general information, not insurance or medical advice. Your policy is what counts - we're happy to help
        you check.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand title="Unsure about your cover?" text="Send us your insurer and policy model with your request; we tell you what to expect before any costs arise. Personal reply in English or German." secondary={{ href: '/en/locations/', label: 'Find a clinic' }} />
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 760px; }
  .prose h2 { font-size: 26px; font-weight: 800; margin: 38px 0 14px; }
  .prose p { font-size: 17px; color: var(--mid); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .prose ul, .prose ol { font-size: 17px; color: var(--mid); padding-left: 22px; margin: 0 0 18px; }
  .prose li { margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .prose strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .tldr {
    background: var(--blue-light);
    border: 1px solid rgba(45, 155, 111, .25);
    border-radius: var(--r-card);
    padding: 20px 22px;
    font-size: 17px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 8px;
  }
  .tldr strong { color: var(--blue-dark); }
  .inline-link { color: var(--blue); font-weight: 700; text-decoration: none; }
  .inline-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .faq details {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--r-btn);
    padding: 4px 18px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  .faq summary {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 14px 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .faq summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .faq summary::after { content: '+'; float: right; color: var(--blue); font-weight: 800; }
  .faq details[open] summary::after { content: '–'; }
  .faq details[open] summary { padding-bottom: 4px; }
  .faq p { margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .cta-row { margin: 32px 0 24px; }
  .disclaimer { font-size: 14px; color: var(--mid); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 18px; }
</style>
